 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "lamentationibus",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "extremis affligebat lamentationibus animum",
   "trans": "she tormented her heart with the most intense laments",
   "body": "Psyche’s grief is heaped up: the superlative <em>extremis lamentationibus</em> (\"with the bitterest of laments\") frames the verb <em>affligebat</em> (\"battered, tormented\"). She does not merely weep — she beats her own heart with sorrow.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The pathos of <em>extremis ... lamentationibus animum affligebat</em> conveys grief as self-inflicted violence: the superlative and the harsh verb show Psyche tormenting her own heart as she watches her husband disappear.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "remigio-plumae",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Epic Metaphor",
   "quote": "remigio plumae raptum",
   "trans": "carried off by the oarage of his wings",
   "body": "The phrase <em>remigium plumae</em> (\"the oarage of his wing\") is a grand, almost epic metaphor — Cupid \"rows\" through the air on his feathers, as a ship is rowed over the sea. The lofty image suits the god’s soaring departure.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The metaphor <em>remigio plumae</em> casts Cupid’s wing-beats as the oar-strokes of a ship, an elevated, almost Virgilian image that dignifies his flight and measures the distance opening between him and Psyche.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "praecipitem-dedit",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Suicidal Despair",
   "quote": "praecipitem sese dedit",
   "trans": "she flung herself headlong",
   "body": "Bereft, Psyche tries to end it all: <em>praecipitem sese dedit</em> — \"she threw herself headlong\" into the river. The violent reflexive recalls her earlier suicide attempt with the blade; grief drives her again to self-destruction.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The phrase <em>praecipitem sese dedit</em> dramatises Psyche’s despair as she flings herself into the river — her second suicide attempt — the headlong reflexive conveying her total abandonment.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "aquas-urere",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Witty Paradox",
   "quote": "qui et ipsas aquas urere consuevit",
   "trans": "who is accustomed to set even the waters themselves on fire",
   "body": "A clever conceit explains why the river spares her: it honours Cupid, the god whose fire of love can <em>even set water itself ablaze</em>. The paradox of fire burning water captures the irresistible, boundary-breaking power of love.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The paradox <em>ipsas aquas urere</em> — Love sets even water on fire — wittily explains the river’s reverence, and through the impossible image asserts the universal, all-conquering power of Cupid.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "mitis-metuens",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Personification",
   "quote": "mitis fluvius ... metuens sibi ... exposuit",
   "trans": "the gentle stream ... fearing for itself ... set her down",
   "body": "The river is fully personified: <em>mitis</em> (\"gentle\"), it is <em>metuens sibi</em> (\"afraid for itself\") of Cupid’s wrath, and so carefully sets Psyche down unharmed on a flowery bank. Even nature conspires to keep her alive for the trials ahead.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The personified <em>mitis fluvius</em>, <em>metuens sibi</em>, gently rescues Psyche — nature itself, fearful of the love-god, refuses to let her die, a touch of humour and a sign that her story is far from over.\"",
   "line": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 8
}